    Section(
      title: '+ What are the SCI-specific risks during pregnancy and labor, and how do you manage autonomic dysreflexia risk during delivery?',
      content:
          '**SCI-Specific Pregnancy Risks**:\n\n**1. Autonomic Dysreflexia During Labor (Most Dangerous Risk)**:\n- **Any patient with SCI at T6 or above is at risk**. T7-T8 patients may also experience AD, particularly with the intense noxious stimulus of uterine contractions.\n- Uterine contractions are the strongest visceral stimulus — can trigger severe, life-threatening AD with BP >200 mmHg.\n- **The patient will not feel contractions** (below T8), so AD symptoms (headache, flushing, sweating above the level) may be the **first sign of labor**.\n- **Management**: Epidural anesthesia is the treatment of choice — it blocks the afferent limb of the AD reflex. Must be placed early — ideally at the onset of labor, before contractions intensify.\n- **Anesthesia team must be aware**: Pre-operative planning with the obstetric anesthesiologist well before delivery. Document the AD risk prominently in the chart.\n- **If AD occurs before epidural placement**: Sit upright, immediate BP monitoring. Nitroglycerin paste or IV hydralazine for acute management. Short-acting antihypertensives — avoid long-acting agents that could cause sustained hypotension (her baseline BP is already 102/64).\n\n**2. Unrecognized Preterm Labor**:\n- She will not feel contractions. Preterm labor could progress undetected.\n- **Monitoring**: Teach self-palpation of uterine tightening. Monitor for increased spasticity (a sign of noxious stimuli below the level). Weekly cervical checks starting at 28 weeks. Home uterine activity monitoring may be considered.\n- Educate on non-pain signs of labor: bloody show, rupture of membranes, rhythmic abdominal tightening, back spasms above T8, AD symptoms.\n\n**3. Urinary Tract Infections**:\n- UTI risk is markedly increased: SCI baseline risk + pregnancy-related urinary stasis + ureteral dilation + growing uterus compressing bladder.\n- **CIC frequency**: Increase as pregnancy progresses. May need every 3-4 hours in the third trimester as bladder capacity decreases.\n- **Screening**: Monthly urine cultures. Treat asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy (unlike in non-pregnant SCI patients).\n- UTIs can trigger preterm labor and AD.\n\n**4. Venous Thromboembolism**:\n- SCI + pregnancy = very high DVT/PE risk.\n- **Prophylaxis**: Low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin) throughout pregnancy. Compression stockings. Frequent position changes.\n- Plan anticoagulation bridge for delivery.\n\n**5. Pressure Injury Risk**:\n- Weight gain (expected 25-35 lbs) alters pressure distribution in wheelchair.\n- Reassess wheelchair cushion quarterly during pregnancy.\n- Increase weight shifts (every 15 minutes).\n- If bed rest is ever required, aggressive pressure prevention protocol.\n\n**6. Respiratory**:\n- T8 — respiratory muscles mostly intact (diaphragm C3-5, intercostals). However, growing uterus elevates diaphragm.\n- Monitor FVC if respiratory symptoms develop. Incentive spirometry.\n\n**Delivery Planning**:\n- **Route**: Vaginal delivery is possible and preferred in most SCI patients. Cesarean section reserved for obstetric indications.\n- She will not feel contractions — will need coaching for pushing. Abdominal muscle paralysis (below T8) means she may have weak expulsive effort. Assisted vaginal delivery (vacuum or forceps) may be needed.\n- **Epidural anesthesia placed early** — for AD prevention, not pain control.\n- **Deliver at a tertiary center** with MFM, obstetric anesthesia, and PM&R available.\n- **Post-partum**: Monitor for AD during uterine involution. DVT prophylaxis. Reassess bladder and bowel programs. Breastfeeding is possible and encouraged — no contraindication from SCI.',
