    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a comprehensive pregnancy management plan for a T8 ASIA A patient, including parenting preparation and postpartum rehabilitation.',
      content:
          '**Trimester-by-Trimester Plan**:\n\n**Second Trimester (Current — 20-28 weeks)**:\n- **MFM co-management**: Monthly visits with maternal-fetal medicine. Growth scans.\n- **PM&R visits**: Every 4-6 weeks to monitor SCI-specific issues.\n- **Bladder**: Increase CIC frequency from every 6 hours to every 4-5 hours as bladder capacity decreases. Monthly urine cultures.\n- **DVT prophylaxis**: Start enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily (if not already). Compression stockings.\n- **Wheelchair/seating**: OT reassessment of cushion pressure mapping with increased weight. May need cushion adjustment or upgrade.\n- **Equipment planning**: Research adaptive parenting equipment — begin ordering now. Include: side-opening crib, wheelchair-accessible changing table, baby carrier adapted for wheelchair use (e.g., wrap-style carriers that secure baby to wheelchair user\'s chest).\n- **Anesthesia consultation**: Meet with obstetric anesthesiologist to discuss epidural planning for delivery.\n\n**Third Trimester (28-40 weeks)**:\n- **Increase monitoring**: MFM visits every 2 weeks from 28 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks.\n- **Cervical checks**: Weekly starting at 28 weeks to detect silent cervical change.\n- **Uterine monitoring**: Teach self-palpation. Consider home uterine activity monitoring.\n- **CIC**: Increase to every 3-4 hours. May become technically more difficult — larger abdomen makes positioning challenging. OT can help problem-solve technique.\n- **Respiratory**: Monitor for dyspnea. FVC if symptomatic.\n- **Wheelchair mobility**: May need tilt-in-space wheelchair temporarily if sitting tolerance decreases.\n- **Hospital bag**: Include catheterization supplies, bowel program supplies, wheelchair cushion, and a written care plan for nursing staff unfamiliar with SCI management.\n- **Preterm labor education**: Review signs — uterine tightening, increased spasticity, AD symptoms, back tightness, rupture of membranes.\n\n**Delivery Plan**:\n- Scheduled admission at 39 weeks (or earlier if indicated).\n- Epidural placed at onset of active labor or scheduled induction.\n- Accessible delivery room — wheelchair access, accessible bed.\n- PM&R physician available for AD management.\n- Neonatology on standby (standard).\n- Written AD protocol posted at bedside.\n\n**Postpartum Plan**:\n- **Bladder**: Reassess as uterus involutes — CIC schedule may return to pre-pregnancy frequency over 6-8 weeks.\n- **Bowel**: Restart bowel program medications that were held during pregnancy.\n- **DVT prophylaxis**: Continue for 6 weeks postpartum.\n- **Breastfeeding**: Encourage — milk production is hormonally driven and unaffected by SCI. Lactation consultant referral. Positioning assistance — side-lying or adapted wheelchair positioning.\n- **Mood**: Screen for postpartum depression at 2, 6, and 12 weeks. Baseline depression risk is elevated in SCI patients.\n- **Parenting OT**: Occupational therapy focused specifically on adaptive parenting — bathing the baby, changing diapers from wheelchair, wheelchair transfers while holding baby, car seat management.\n- **Home health**: Home nursing visit in first week to assess safety and provide support.\n\n**Adaptive Parenting Equipment**:\n- **Side-opening crib**: Allows wheelchair-level access without reaching over a railing.\n- **Wheelchair-height changing table**: Or use bed/couch for changing.\n- **Baby carrier**: Front-carry system that secures baby to chest with straps around wheelchair back. Keeps hands free for wheelchair propulsion.\n- **Stroller attachment**: Devices that attach a baby stroller to the wheelchair frame.\n- **Bath support**: Baby bathtub placed on a table at wheelchair height.',
