    Section(
      title: '+ How do you ensure coordinated, expert care when most team members have never managed a pregnant SCI patient?',
      content:
          '**Care Coordination Strategy**:\n\n**1. Designate PM&R as Co-Lead**:\n- The physiatrist should serve as **co-lead with MFM** throughout the pregnancy.\n- PM&R provides the SCI expertise that OB/MFM lacks; MFM provides the obstetric expertise that PM&R lacks.\n- Every decision should be made collaboratively.\n\n**2. Create a Shared Written Care Plan**:\n- A single document accessible to all providers (in the EHR as a shared care plan or problem list note) containing:\n  - SCI level, ASIA classification, baseline BP and vitals\n  - AD risk, triggers, and acute management protocol\n  - Bladder program (CIC schedule, supplies)\n  - Bowel program\n  - Skin management (pressure prevention)\n  - DVT prophylaxis plan\n  - Labor detection plan (she cannot feel contractions)\n  - Delivery plan (early epidural, AD monitoring)\n  - Postpartum plan\n- This document should be updated at each visit and available to any provider or nurse who sees the patient.\n\n**3. Multidisciplinary Planning Conference**:\n- At 28-30 weeks, convene a planning meeting: PM&R, MFM, OB, obstetric anesthesia, labor and delivery nursing, OT, and the patient/husband.\n- Review the delivery plan in detail. Address questions. Run through AD scenarios.\n- This meeting ensures everyone is aligned and no assumptions are made.\n\n**4. Labor and Delivery Staff Education**:\n- Many L&D nurses have never managed an SCI patient. Provide targeted education:\n  - AD recognition and management (this is an emergency — they must know the protocol)\n  - Neurogenic bladder — continue CIC during labor, do not assume she can void\n  - She will not report contraction pain — use tocodynamometer and palpation\n  - Skin: Reposition every 2 hours during labor. Use pressure-relieving mattress overlay.\n  - Transfer assistance — she can direct her own transfers\n- Laminated AD protocol card at bedside during labor.\n\n**5. Anesthesia Preparation**:\n- The anesthesiologist must understand that epidural is for **AD prevention**, not just pain control.\n- Epidural should be placed early, before active labor intensifies.\n- Have IV labetalol and nitroglycerin immediately available.\n- Post-anesthesia: Monitor for AD during uterine involution (24-48 hours postpartum).\n\n**6. Patient as Expert**:\n- This patient knows her body and her SCI better than any of her providers. Include her as an active participant in all planning. Her self-knowledge is a clinical asset.\n\n**7. Documentation for Future Pregnancies/Patients**:\n- After delivery, document the entire experience (complications, what worked, what did not) as a case report or institutional protocol.\n- This builds institutional knowledge for future SCI pregnancies.',
