    Section(
      title: '+ How do you respond to a family member who openly questions a disabled woman\'s right to become a mother, while supporting the patient and preserving family relationships?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Address the patient first — she was just hurt, and she is your patient.**\n[Turn to the patient and make eye contact]\n"Before I respond to what was just said, I want to say something directly to you. You are going to be a wonderful mother. Your ability to parent has nothing to do with whether you can walk. I work with parents with disabilities every day, and I have seen extraordinary parenting from people who use wheelchairs, who are blind, who are deaf, and who have every kind of physical limitation you can imagine. The qualities that make someone a good parent — love, patience, creativity, dedication — have nothing to do with legs."\n\n**2. Turn to the mother-in-law with respect, not hostility.**\n"Mrs. [Name], I can hear that you are coming from a place of love and concern — both for your son and for your future grandchild. Those concerns deserve a response, not dismissal. So let me address them specifically."\n\n**3. Address each concern with facts, not emotion.**\n"You asked how a woman in a wheelchair will take care of a baby. The answer is: with planning, adaptive equipment, and the same problem-solving skills she uses to manage everything else in her life. There are specially designed cribs that open from the side at wheelchair height. There are baby carriers made for wheelchair users. There are changing tables, bath supports, and feeding setups all designed for parents with disabilities. We have an occupational therapist who will work with her specifically on every parenting task — from diaper changes to bath time to car seat transfers. She will not do things the same way a standing parent does, but she will do them."\n\n"You asked about emergencies — what if there is a fire. That is a fair question, and it is one we plan for. We develop emergency evacuation plans with every family. And I would point out that her apartment is ground-floor and accessible — she chose it intentionally."\n\n**4. Gently but firmly challenge the underlying assumption.**\n"But I want to address something deeper in what you said. You said it is not fair to bring a child into this situation, and that your son should have married someone who could give him a normal family. I say this respectfully, but I disagree with that premise. Research consistently shows that children of parents with disabilities develop normally. In fact, many studies show they develop greater empathy, resilience, and problem-solving skills. A \'normal\' family is a family where a child is loved, safe, and supported. This child will have all of those things."\n\n**5. Name the legal and ethical reality without being adversarial.**\n"I also want to mention that the right to have children is a fundamental human right. The Americans with Disabilities Act and reproductive rights law are very clear: disability does not disqualify someone from parenthood. Your daughter-in-law\'s decision to have this baby is her decision, supported by her husband, and it is a decision I wholeheartedly support as her physician."\n\n**6. Invite the mother-in-law into the solution rather than the opposition.**\n"Mrs. [Name], your grandchild is coming in about 20 weeks. That child is going to need a grandmother who believes in this family. Instead of worrying about what your daughter-in-law cannot do, I would love to see you channel that energy into helping her prepare. Help her set up the nursery. Come to an OT session and see how the adaptive equipment works. I think if you see her in action, your worries will ease. She is one of the most capable people I have met."\n\n**7. Check in with the husband.**\n"[Husband\'s name], I want to make sure you are heard too. How are you feeling about all of this?"\n[Allow him to express support for his wife, which reinforces the family unit.]\n\n**8. Return to the patient and reaffirm.**\n"[Patient\'s name], you planned this pregnancy. You prepared for it. You are managing it beautifully. Twenty weeks from now, you are going to hold your baby, and all of this worry will become love. I am here to make sure you have every resource and every tool you need. Let us get back to planning."\n\n**Key Principles**: Prioritized the patient\'s emotional well-being first. Addressed concerns with specific facts rather than dismissing them. Challenged ableist assumptions respectfully but firmly. Cited evidence about outcomes for children of disabled parents. Named reproductive rights without being legalistic. Invited the mother-in-law to become part of the solution. Affirmed the husband\'s role. Returned focus to the patient\'s agency and competence.',
